expect: written
summary: needed
keeps: undefined | nothing | no reason | none | accept
bars: \bwhich\b[^.]*\bwhich\b | \bwhose\b[^.]*\bwhich\b | \bwhose\b[^.,]*\bthe catalog lists\b | \bits catalog entry\b | \bmismatchof\b
why: Kirill found a summary with a relative clause inside a relative clause hard to read; a summary holds one relative clause at most, so what the function returns and when it returns nothing each stay in one clause
labelled: 2026-09-30
--- code
export function mismatchOf(statement: Statement, catalog: Catalog): string | undefined {
  for (const line of statement.lines) {
    const listed = catalog.closingBalance(line.account);
    if (listed !== line.closingBalance) {
      return `${line.account}: closing balance ${line.closingBalance} is not the listed ${listed}`;
    }
  }
  return undefined;
}
--- facts
The import refuses a statement for the first line whose closing balance is not the balance the catalog lists for that line's account, and shows the operator this reason.
A statement with no such line is accepted.
